-Every ordinary window opens floating at its preferred size. Oversized automatic windows are reduced proportionally to fit the usable area, including title bars and margins. Client-driven size changes keep automatic placement centered until you move the window. New windows start at the center and shift diagonally when they would completely cover another window whose upper-left corner is visible. Placement includes title bars and borders. The offset clears the decorated title bar, with a minimum of 32 logical pixels, and wraps to fresh diagonals at the output boundary, including when several windows open simultaneously. Dialogs center over their parent; explicit position rules take precedence.
+Every ordinary window opens floating at the configured default size, `default_width_fraction` by `default_height_fraction` of the usable area, and joins the cascade. A window that opens directly in stacking looks the same as one that arrived when a tiled workspace switched to stacking. New windows start at the center and shift diagonally when they would completely cover another window whose upper-left corner is visible. Placement includes title bars and borders. The offset clears the decorated title bar, with a minimum of 32 logical pixels, and wraps to fresh diagonals at the output boundary, including when several windows open simultaneously. A size set by an explicit window rule, or remembered from an earlier floating episode, still wins. Dialogs keep their own size and center over their parent; explicit position rules take precedence.
+
+When a window leaves a tiling layout for floating, its first floating episode does not keep the tile size: a lone tile can fill the output, and floating it would stay full-screen. Instead it takes `default_width_fraction` by `default_height_fraction` of the usable area, then joins the cascade. A window that has floated before keeps the size and position you last gave it, including a manual resize. Switching a whole workspace from a tiling layout to stacking lays its windows out in cascade order, so they do not pile up on top of each other, and new windows open there with the same size and cascade.
+
+```toml
+[layout.stacking]
+default_width_fraction = 0.6
+default_height_fraction = 0.6
+cascade_step = 0
+```
+
+| Key                       | Default | Description                                                                                                                                                                |
+| ------------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `default_width_fraction`  | `0.6`   | Width of a window's first floating episode, whether it opens in stacking or leaves tiling, as a fraction of the usable area. Later episodes keep the remembered size. Override per workspace with `layout.stacking.default_width_fraction`.  |
+| `default_height_fraction` | `0.6`   | Height of a window's first floating episode, whether it opens in stacking or leaves tiling, as a fraction of the usable area. Override per workspace with `layout.stacking.default_height_fraction`.                       |
+| `cascade_step`            | `0`     | Minimum diagonal offset between cascaded windows, in logical pixels. `0` derives it from the title bar, never below 32 px. Override per workspace with `layout.stacking.cascade_step`. |
 
 Enable title bars to move windows without a modifier and use their close and maximize buttons. Dragging updates the window position continuously, so maximize and restore use its current position even while the pointer is held. Region placement animations start where you release the window and keep the normal restore box from before the drag. The configured `appearance.drag_opacity` still applies.
 
 Directional focus uses window geometry, including overlapping windows. `window-toggle-floating` is rejected in this layout. Switching to a tiling layout re-tiles windows floated by the layout, while windows explicitly floated by a rule or before entering this layout stay floating. Moving a layout-floated window to a tiling workspace also tiles it there.
 
+A tiling mode remembers its arrangement when the workspace leaves it. A round trip through stacking (or through another tiling mode) puts each window back in its old tile: dwindle keeps its splits and split ratios, master keeps its master and stack rows, and scrolling keeps its columns and widths. Windows opened meanwhile join afterwards; a window that closed while away leaves an empty slot that the layout collapses like any other close.
+
+Floating a single tiled window also keeps the tile it left on that workspace. Tiling it again returns it to that slot with the earlier splits or column width, provided the rest of the arrangement has not changed; otherwise it joins like a new window. Pinning the floating window keeps that record while it follows other workspaces, so returning to the workspace it came from and unpinning or tiling it there still restores its slot. The record is discarded when the window is unpinned on a different workspace, when it moves there unpinned, or when it closes.
+
